OpenStax's own source HTML has no space between the word "of" and the
immediately following inline math span containing $1$ (the exercise's
"...a mass of<math>1</math> kg weighs $9.8$ N near the surface of the Earth.)
-- a single missing whitespace text node in the source, not a converter bug
(no other math span in this page's exercises abuts adjacent text with no
separating space). Restored by hand as a literal space between "of" and
"$1$".

Manual review corrections (math-mode boundaries, redundant delimiters, and
one upstream mathematical error):

- Five one-dimensional-density exercises trapped the closing parenthesis of
  "(starting at x=...)" inside the adjacent math span (e.g. "$x=0)$"). Moved
  the parenthesis outside the math span in each case ("$x=0$)").
- Simplified the oversized `\left( 1/2 \right)` delimiters around the trivial
  exponent `1/2` in the metal-rod density function to plain parentheses,
  matching the plain `(1/2)x^{2}` notation already used two exercises later.
- In the rocket/gravity exercise, removed a redundant outer parenthesis pair
  around the already-parenthesized numerator in `F=-((GMm)/x^{2})`, matching
  the file's established single-parenthesized-numerator convention (compare
  the cone-tank exercise's `A=(\pi r^{2}h^{2})/H^{2}`), and moved the
  sentence-ending period out of the trailing math span
  `\text{kg}.)$` -> `\text{kg}$.)` so the closing parenthesis of the "(Note:
  ...)" aside is not glued to math.
- Corrected an upstream mathematical error in the final cone-shaped-tank
  exercise: the cross-sectional-area formula was printed as
  `A=(\pi r^{2}h^{2})/H^{3}` (confirmed against the live OpenStax page and
  its LibreTexts mirror, both of which independently print `H^{3}`), but the
  exercise's own claim -- "the work to empty it is half the work for a
  cylinder with the same height and base" -- only holds with `H^{2}`.
  Dimensional/physical consistency requires it too: evaluating the area at
  the tank's full depth, A(H), must reduce to a fixed base area independent
  of H (matching the comparison cylinder's constant base); that holds only
  when the H exponent equals the h exponent (2), not 3. Direct integration
  of the pumping work with `H^{2}` reproduces the stated 1:2 work ratio
  exactly; `H^{3}` does not. Corrected to `H^{2}`.

